    "Final Project Step 2 - Statistical Analysis Plan\n",
    "Name: Gahyun Lim (Josie)\n",
    "\n",
    "Dataset: Center for Health Discovery and Well Being (CHDWB)  \n",
    "\n",
    "Research Questions:\n",
    "1.\tTo identify food items or food groups that are associated with circulating delta-valerobetaine (VB) levels.\n",
    "2.\tTo examine whether the associations between circulating VB and the identified food items/groups differ across age groups.\n",
    "3.\tTo investigate sex-specific differences in the associations between circulating VB and the identified food items/groups.\n",
    "\n",
    "Data Analysis Steps:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Modify datasets.\n",
    "•\tFood Frequency Questionnaire (FFQ) data were manually converted to numeric values, as each food item used a different response scale and frequency category. \n",
    "•\tThe circulating VB levels were measured using Liquid Chromatography-High Resolution Mass Spectrometry (LC-HRMS), yielding the VB intensity.\n",
    "•\tThere are two datasets: one containing continuous variables from the FFQ and VB intensity, and the other containing categorical variables from the FFQ and VB intensity.\n",
    "\n",
    "2. Upload the dataset to the Python virtual environment. \n",
    "Uploaded two datasets (1. Continuous / 2. Categorical) to the Python virtual environment, into the folder named ‘Final Project - Josie’. After food items/group identification, a combined dataset of demographics, biochemical, and food items/groups will be uploaded for convenience.\n",
    "\n",
    "3. Statistical Analysis Plan (SAP) \n",
    "The primary goal of this project is to identify food items/groups that are associated with circulating VB levels. Additionally, the project aims to examine whether these associations differ according to age groups and sex.\n",
    "\n",
    "Step 1: Identify food items/groups associated with the circulating VB.\n",
    "\n",
    "•\tVB intensity (continuous) vs. food items/groups (continuous): \n",
    "Associations will be evaluated using Spearman’s rank correlation, a non-parametric method suitable for metabolomics and dietary intake data, which are typically non-normally distributed. Both correlation coefficients (ρ) and corresponding p-values will be reported.\n",
    "•\tVB intensity (continuous) vs. food items/groups (categorical): Differences in VB intensity across categories will be evaluated using the  Kruskal–Wallis test with pairwise Mann–Whitney (Wilcoxon rank-sum), which does not assume normal distribution.\n",
    "Two tables listing the food items/groups identified will be provided. \n",
    "\n",
    "Step 2: Evaluate subgroup-specific associations\n",
    "\n",
    "Identified food items/groups will be further analyzed to assess whether their associations with circulating VB levels differ by age groups and sex. Covariates will not be included in this project. \n",
    "\n",
    "•\tStratified Descriptive Statistics: Summary statistics will be calculated separately within each subgroup. For continuous variables, mean ± SD or median [IQR] will be reported, and for categorical variables, counts and percentages will be reported.\n",
    "•\tExample Model: ln (δ-VB) = β0 + β1s (Beef Intake) + ϵ [s = f (females), m (males)]\n",
    "\n",
    "Bar plots showing VB intensity by age group and sex will be provided, along with a table summarizing subgroup-specific association statistics. All analyses will be conducted in Python using pandas, numpy, scipy.stats, statsmodels, and matplotlib.\n"
   ]
  }
 ],
